@page "/Projects/SiepieAndTakkieDialogSystem"

<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/SiepieAndTakkieDialogSystem" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <div>
      <h3>Project description</h3>
      <span>
        The dialog system is made for the game
        <NavLink href="/Projects/SiepieAndTakkieOriginal">Siepie and Takkie</NavLink>. It loads dialog per scene
        based on the file name and location.
        If it has loaded dialog it can display it and manage the conversation flow.
      </span>
    </div>
    <h3>How to use it</h3>
    <span>
      In the scene where you want to use the dialog system attach the InitDialogSystem component to an empty
      GameObject.
      From then on every scene is checked for a corresponding JSON file in the default path:
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/DefaultFilePathDialogSystem.png" />
    <span>
      The InitDialogSystem calls DontDestroyOnLoad(this.gameObject) so the per-scene loading works correctly.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@DontDestroyOnLoad" />
    <span>
      This also means you should destroy it externally if you don't intend on keeping it around.
      As seen in the void Start() the InitDialogSystem subscribes to the SceneManager.activeSceneChanged in order to
      listen for scene changes.
      When the scene changes it gets the new path for the json and then sets it as the new path for the Deserializer.
      The Deserializer itself gets the actual JSON file through the Resources class.
      Then when GetDeserializedObject gets called it returns the generic with which the class was instantiated.
      In this case it's the DialogData object.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@DialogData" />
    <span>
      Make sure that the scene JSON is properly set up.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@UID" />
    <span>
      UID is the unique identifier that binds the dialog to the npc (in order to bind it you need to set the UID for the
      NpcDialogPreparer on the target NPC to the same number).
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@SequenceID" />
    <span>
      This is the identifier NpcDialogPreparer uses to iterate over the dialog. The order of entries in the JSON
      doesn't matter as long as you assign the sequence using this field.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@Speaker" />
    <span>
      The character who's actually speaking should be put here. The keyword "PLAYER" will be replaced with the name
      of the character that interacted with the NPC. The dialog property should be self-explanatory; it's the text
      the characters will say.<br />
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@JsonExampleDialogData" />
    <span>
      DialogData is used to feed data to the NpcDialogPreparer. This class is called when NpcInteract receives
      an interaction request from the PlayerInteractor. NpcDialogPreparer instantiates the DialogTemplatePrefab
      and keeps a reference to the UIDialogSequenceManager that's in the prefab.
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/DialogTemplatePrefab.png" />
    <span>You should see something like this if you open it:</span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/UXMLVisual.png" />
    <span>
      The UIDialogSequenceManager is the layer that directly controls this UI. It queries the UXML document for
      its elements and searches for the tags that were set up in the UXML document.
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/UXMLTags.png" />
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@UIDialogSequenceManagerQuery" />
    <span>
      Once all references and instances are in place, NpcDialogPreparer keeps listening to NpcInteract to know
      when to iterate through the dialog lines until the dialog is finished.<br />
      This is my result:
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/TakkieTakkieDialog.gif" />

    <h3>Unit testing</h3>
    <span>I was new to testing and had no real clue how a unit test looked. I spent time reviewing different testing
      frameworks. Because using a framework seemed to be the norm for writing unit tests. That raised two questions: why
      use a
      testing framework, and what are the differences between them?</span>
    <h3>What I learnt</h3>
    <span>Testing frameworks are used because they can handle things for you.
      They give more detailed reports on test results, have built-in assertions, and generally have some sort of editor
      integration.
      Most of the time they also have syntax that encourages the AAA pattern which makes tests easier to read and
      modify.
      So it's not necessary to use a testing framework, but it can make writing and maintaining tests easier.
      As for the question about differences between frameworks I compared NUnit, MSTest and xUnit.
      There weren't any significant differences in my eyes.
      MSTest was integrated with Visual Studio but lacked some features that the other two had.
      xUnit was the recommendation of many people with it's more modern syntax and small library but it wasn't
      integrated with Unity's testing framework.
      So I settled on NUnit.
    </span>
    <img src="images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/FailingPassingTests.png" />
    <h3>What I ended up doing</h3>
    <span> For this project I applied the test driven development pattern.
      That meant writing unit tests for every class before writing the actual implementation of the class.
      I wrote tests for as many of the dialog system classes possible. Take the Deserializer class here for example:
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@UnitTest" />
    <span>
      Writing the tests first made me look differently at the implementation of the classes.
      I had to make sure the classes were testable which meant classes had to be more modular, accessible and have less
      dependencies.
      It was interesting to learn and I will definitely use unit testing in my bigger projects.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>


